  <!-- Deep Dive 2: Cranelift JIT Compiler -->
  <section id="cranelift-jit" class="section-block">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="deep-dive-card card glass-card animate-fade-up">
        <div class="deep-dive-header">
          <div class="header-meta">
            <span class="badge badge-red">In-Memory JIT Compilation</span>
            <span class="badge">Chunks 1–4, 12, 14</span>
            <span class="badge font-mono">src/jit.rs &bull; cranelift 0.136.2</span>
          </div>
          <h2>Cranelift 0.136.2 JIT SSA Engine</h2>
          <p class="deep-dive-lead">
            High-speed in-memory Just-In-Time machine code generation targeting x86_64 and AArch64 with automatic call-frequency tiering and deoptimization fallbacks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="deep-dive-grid">
          <div class="deep-dive-text">
            <h3>Tiering Threshold: JIT_THRESHOLD = 20</h3>
            <p>
              Shae employs an adaptive tiering strategy. During initial script execution, functions run on the lightweight Bytecode VM with zero compilation latency. Once any compiled function reaches <code>JIT_THRESHOLD = 20</code> invocations, the VM background-compiles its bytecode into Cranelift SSA IR.
            </p>
            <h3>Direct C Function Pointer Execution</h3>
            <p>
              Compiled native code is emitted directly into executable memory pages and invoked via the standard C calling convention:
              <code>unsafe extern "C" fn(*const i64, usize, *mut i64) -&gt; i32</code>.
              This completely bypasses virtual stack dispatch, opcode fetch-decode loops, and interpreter call overhead.
            </p>
            <h3>Native Float SSA &amp; Deoptimization Fallback</h3>
            <p>
              Numerical operations use Cranelift's native 64-bit float registers (<code>types::F64</code>) and integer registers (<code>types::I64</code>). If dynamic types diverge at runtime or an unsupported opcode is encountered, execution seamlessly falls back to the Bytecode VM interpreter.
            </p>

